## Example Usage

Primitive types carry no `schema_data`:

```terraform
resource "pulsar_schema" "string" {
  tenant    = "public"
  namespace = "default"
  topic     = "string-events"
  type      = "STRING"
}
```

Complex types require `schema_data`. Note that `JSON` schemas are declared with an Avro-style
record definition, the same shape as `AVRO` — not as a JSON Schema document:

```terraform
resource "pulsar_schema" "avro" {
  tenant      = "public"
  namespace   = "default"
  topic       = "user-events-avro"
  type        = "AVRO"
  schema_data = jsonencode({
    "type" : "record",
    "name" : "User",
    "fields" : [
      { "name" : "name", "type" : "string" },
      { "name" : "age", "type" : "int" }
    ]
  })

  properties = {
    "author" = "terraform"
  }
}

resource "pulsar_schema" "json" {
  tenant      = "public"
  namespace   = "default"
  topic       = "user-events-json"
  type        = "JSON"
  schema_data = jsonencode({
    "type" : "record",
    "name" : "User",
    "fields" : [
      { "name" : "name", "type" : "string" },
      { "name" : "age", "type" : "int" }
    ]
  })
}
```

## Import

Schemas can be imported using the format `tenant/namespace/topic`:

```shell
terraform import {{.Name}}.example my-tenant/my-namespace/my-topic
```

## Notes

- Updates create new schema versions.
- Complex types require `schema_data`; primitive types do not.
- `tenant`, `namespace`, and `topic` identify a persistent topic and cannot be changed in place.
